[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: SingleChoice and MultipleChoice options are quoted by different rules on read and on write

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-MULTIPLECHOICE-QUOTING-ASYMMETRY
TITLE: SingleChoice and MultipleChoice options are quoted by different rules on read and on write
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on 0.28.3 while writing the .sgra emitter. StrictDoc's reader and
writer treat SingleChoice and MultipleChoice options differently, and only
SingleChoice is self-consistent.

GrammarElementFieldSingleChoice strips a surrounding double quote from every
option at construction, and get_unprocessed_options re-adds it on write for any
option containing a parenthesis. GrammarElementFieldMultipleChoice does
neither: the model keeps whatever quoting the file carried, and the writer joins
options verbatim.

Two consequences. A MultipleChoice option holding a parenthesis, built
programmatically without quotes, is written to a file that does not parse --
verified, it fails with a TextXSyntaxError expecting a separator or a closing
parenthesis at the inner one. And a correctly quoted one round-trips
byte-stable but carries the literal quote characters inside the model's option
string, so a node's field value has to include them to match.

The emitter quotes parenthesized options for both kinds, because that is the
only spelling the grammar accepts. The residual asymmetry is in the model value
and is upstream's.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed because the emitter's quoting rule was specified as what strictdoc's own
writer does, and the writer does two different things depending on the field
kind. Worth knowing before any consumer compares an option vocabulary against a
field value, and before anyone treats strictdoc's writer as the byte-identity
oracle for a grammar that uses MultipleChoice.
<<<
